        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[The Future Isn’t Evenly Distributed]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[By analysing the request for support on a 25-year-old Perl CGI script that he recently got, Dave Cross explains why developers’ apps are usually lagging behind in technology. Developers should realise that technological advancements do not solely depend on innovations in technology but also on economic factors. Indeed, businesses usually do not require the latest tools, they seek a way to make yesterday’s solutions work today.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.08.01</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://perlhacks.com/2026/08/the-future-isnt-evenly-distributed/</guid>
            <link>https://perlhacks.com/2026/08/the-future-isnt-evenly-distributed/</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[dave_cross]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Immutable Class]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[In this post, I analyse the treatment of immutability in contemporary Perl by comparing and contrasting the explicit runtime optimisation provided by Moose to the experimental class features offered by core Perl concerning automatic enforcement of immutability from an external perspective.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.07.26</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/immutable-class</guid>
            <link>https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/immutable-class</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[mohammad_anwar]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Reflection/MOP]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Here I document the evolution of code reflection and metaobject protocols from Lisp and CLOS all the way to contemporary object systems. And finally turn the attention to how Perl has evolved—starting with the first implementation of a symbol table with %main:: and the Moose framework's use of Class::MOP, and now to features that have become intrinsic to the language like the isa operator, __CLASS__ and capability testing via $obj->can().]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.07.31</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/reflection-or-mop/</guid>
            <link>https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/reflection-or-mop/</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[mohammad_anwar]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[CatalystNext: What Should We Keep, Change, and Rethink? ]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[John Napiorkowski starts the conversation on CatalystNext, which refers to a new concept associated with the old Catalyst Perl web framework on the basis of PAGI technology. In this article, he weighs the benefits of keeping action-subroutine attributes rather than using route DSL, improving the syntax of the chained dispatch method, separating code bundles from URLs, and improving state/service boundaries while avoiding a fundamental issue of historical compatibility.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.07.28</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://dev.to/jjn1056/catalystnext-what-should-we-keep-change-and-rethink-39kf</guid>
            <link>https://dev.to/jjn1056/catalystnext-what-should-we-keep-change-and-rethink-39kf</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[john_napiorkowski]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Moving Targets - PetaPerl 0.6.8]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[PetaMem has launched the new version 0.6.8 of pperl, increasing its minimum version requirement from now on to Perl 5.44. Some of the main highlights of the release include an implemented zero-setup native DBI that allows for PostgreSQL and MySQL support via FFI without any need for XS compiling.]]></description>
